<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mbandzeni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1855–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dlamini IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mbandzeni was king of the Swazi from 1875 until his death in 1889. During his reign he granted large numbers of concessions to white speculators and settlers, covering grazing and farming land, minerals, customs and even the right to run public services, in return for payments. The concessions undermined Swazi independence and became the basis for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and British control of the kingdom in the 1890s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mbandzeni was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a> and Nandzi Nkambule, who died when he was very young. After Mswati's death in July 1868 the Queen Mother Tsandzile Ndwandwe acted as regent, and Ludvonga II, the son of Mswati and Inkhosikati LaMgangeni Khumalo, was chosen as king. Ludvonga died of poisoning in 1874 before he could take the throne. The council of elders asked LaKhumalo to choose another child to become her son, and she chose the motherless Mbandzeni, making him king and herself queen mother. A rival claimant, Prince Mbilini waMswati, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>, never won the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mbandzeni ruled from his royal capital at Mbekelweni, where many concessionaires came seeking land, mining rights and trade. He and his officials granted overlapping concessions to British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> interests, paid in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> or other goods, often on the advice of 'Offy' Shepstone, nephew of Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-shepstone">Theophilus Shepstone</a> of Natal. He could not read or write and signed with a cross, and he was told that the land grants were not permanent, though the papers said otherwise. The discovery of gold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> sharpened the settlers' interest.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> war of 1879 Mbandzeni refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a>'s request for help, since Zulu forces often raided southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>, and Swazi troops helped stop Zulu raids into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>. That year a large Swazi force backed the British under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> against the Pedi king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sekhukhune-i">Sekhukhune</a>, who was captured and his kingdom destroyed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in return recognised Swazi independence and promised protection against Zulu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> encroachment, but the concessions were later used against that independence in the Swaziland conventions of 1884 and 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mbandzeni died after an illness during the border talks, in which he had little say, and is quoted as saying on his deathbed that &quot;the Swazi kingship dies with me&quot;. He was buried at the royal cemetery of Mbilaneni beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-i">Sobhuza I</a>. After a regency under Queen Tibati Nkambule his young son Mahlokohla, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwane-v">Ngwane V</a>, became king. The conventions fixed a border that left many Swazi, including royal villages such as Mjindini, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Some have said that he sold his country; others that he did what he could at a difficult time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mbandzeni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The concessions of <b>Mbandzeni</b>’s reign became a well-known example of how European speculators acquired African land and rights with little regard for local understanding.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1886 a group of Boer farmers who had obtained grazing and land concessions from the Swazi king <b>Mbandzeni</b> in the southwest of his kingdom set themselves up as an independent republic, the Little Free State, around the area of the later town of Piet Retief.</blockquote><a href="/state/klein-vrystaat">Klein Vrystaat</a></li></ul></section>
